y COMPLETE row has no evidence entry whose artifacts resolve in `git ls-tree -r <ref>` AND whose hex tree_fp resolves via `git cat-file -t` to a git object and is not all zeros; it exits 0 when every complete row has one such entry. Re-run today at origin/main it must exit 1 naming at least the 20 row ids in this row's reasoning (CF-009,010,011,012,013,014,015,030,032,033,035,036,037,038,039,041,052,054,058,PERF-012); after the amendments it must exit 0. Paste both invocations and both exit codes into the evidence artifact.\n\n2. The audit is wired into tests/selftest.sh as its own numbered section and the wiring is proven causal: with the pre-fix board (origin/main at b430bed, or a copy of it) the section prints FAIL naming >=20 row ids and the run exits nonzero; with the amended board it prints PASS. Record both runs, including the section's own output lines, in the evidence artifact.\n\n3. The shared fingerprint 59f476975880e46d49eec1fbaec75b289ced048f4e669855d2cee386061fb5ec is retired the documented way for all eight rows that carry it (CF-009, CF-010, CF-014, CF-015, CF-030, CF-032, CF-033, CF-035): each row either gains an appended evidence entry whose fingerprint `git cat-file -t <fp>` resolves to a tree, or records `none:<reason>` PLUS the exact command (algorithm) that regenerates the value at HEAD and the value it regenerates to. A bare re-paste of the same unresolvable digest does not satisfy this criterion.\n\n4. CF-032's second evidence entry stops resting on an unpublished ref: its run_id either names a commit that `git cat-file -t` resolves in a FRESH clone of origin (not only via refs/gitreins/history in the owner's checkout) or is superseded by an appended entry with `none:<reason>`, and its empty artifacts[] is replaced by at least one repository-relative artifact that `git cat-file -e <ref>:<path>` resolves.\n\n5. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS at the closing commit, and `jq -c . .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl` parses every line with no duplicate ids; the closing commit is pushed and the row's commit_hash names it.","created_at":"2026-11-03T15:02:06Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T15:49:59Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"223a2eab36ad3380a974a18b4e9a93341fc680782194f5fb5fdf9fcf27b83269"},"foreman_note":"REVIEW 2026-11-03 15:39 (code-factory-review-2026-11-03-15-39-07) — the first repair in this class landed (91f3a64, sync lane) and it still fails the rule: CF-011 and CF-013 each gained a second evidence entry (run_id sync-20261103-cf011 / sync-20261103-cf013) whose tree_fp is e3f26bdf69b3b8e65f8de6dff285dc95097c436a7dcef786e4d88a8b0a6ee09b, and `git cat-file -t` answers \"fatal: Not a valid object name\" in a fresh clone of origin/main. The artifact path it adds (docs/audits/code-factory-sync-2026-11-03-evidence-rebind.md) does resolve, and that doc states the correct tree e0e7d250bcf9661cd924ab82fa586e419866081d == d4eec41^{tree}, so the right value was known and not written. e3f26bdf is not the tree of d4eec41, e21f2ce, 91f3a64 or 6af06ef, nor sha256 of the d4eec41 tree bytes. The rebind also keeps the original unresolvable entry on purpose, so a gate worded \"any complete row lacks a resolvable entry\" cannot pass CF-011/CF-013 until the historical entry is retired (with a note saying why) or the gate is worded per-entry. Implement the gate per-entry, with an explicit retired-entry allowance, and make the red arm cover a NEW entry carrying an unresolvable fingerprint."}
 {"id":"CF-075","title":"P2 — node-create.sh stages project-node.sh without scripts/disk_floor.py, so every documented node creation dies on its first line with a raw python3 error (and leaves the spawned agent alive)","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-03 by the code-factory-dogfood lane at origin/main b430bed. scripts/node-create.sh:43-47 copies exactly three files into the node (~/node-scripts/project-node.sh, lib.sh, harness/install-<h>.sh); scripts/project-node.sh:64 resolves the disk floor by running \"$HERE/disk_floor.py\", which is a script added after that staging list was written, so a node never receives it. Live run on a reachable host: `BUNKER_SERVER=bunker-las-03 scripts/node-create.sh --project cf-dogfood-1100 --repo https://github.com/github/gitignore --harness hermes --ttl 1h` -> `PASS spawn — node-cf-dogfood-1100-100225`, `PASS staged — project-node.sh + harness/install-hermes.sh`, then inside the node `python3: can't open file '/home/bunker-node-cf-dogfood-1100-100225/node-scripts/disk_floor.py': [Errno 2] No such file or directory` / `NODE: FAIL (disk floor)` / `FAIL provision — node reported FAIL` / `FAIL gateway_reachable ... -> 000000` / `VERIFY: FAIL (1)` / `NODE INCOMPLETE (2)`, rc=1. The identical failure reproduces with no bunker host at all by staging exactly node-create's file set into a temp dir (see probe), so the documented create path cannot produce a working node at this tip regardless of host. The message also breaks the repo's own output convention (AGENTS.md conventions: 'PASS/FAIL/WARN with the measured value'): project-node.sh:63-68 does `say \"$FLOOR\"` and prints the raw interpreter error, so the disk-floor layer never emits a named FAIL row. Second-order defect on the same path: the failed create leaves the spawned agent ALIVE — node-create.sh:63-71 reports FAIL and exits 1 without destroying it; this tick had to run `bunker destroy node-cf-dogfood-1100-100225 --server bunker-las-03` by hand (home 246.7 MB) and a follow-up `bunker info` census returned not_found. Distinct from CF-056 (node-create stage-2 cannot FAIL because cp exit statuses are swallowed): here every cp succeeds and the staged SET is incomplete, so an exit-status fix alone would not notice this. Distinct from CF-041 (quick-start env/arg defects, closed) and CF-040 (build cache).","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree from the repo root: the `probe` field prints `python3: can't open file '.../disk_floor.py': [Errno 2] No such file or directory` followed by `NODE: FAIL (disk floor)` and exits 1 today.\n1. node-create.sh stages every file project-node.sh executes at runtime — at minimum disk_floor.py — and the staged set is derived rather than hand-maintained (copy the scripts the entry point references, or stage scripts/ as a unit). A check on the staged set exits nonzero when any referenced file is missing; `grep -c disk_floor.py scripts/node-create.sh` is non-zero and a selftest fixture fails if it ever returns to 0.\n2. The hermetic staging probe continues past the disk floor and prints a NAMED row for it (PASS or WARN with the measured GiB and its source), never a bare interpreter error, and the run reaches the remaining layers.\n3. A live node creation against a reachable host shows no 'can't open file' line; its fail structure matches the control-host run (crier_identity and inference-key rows may still FAIL — this row is about staging, not about a node being complete).\n4. A failed provision leaves no live agent behind: node-create.sh destroys the agent it spawned when the run ends FAIL, or prints the exact `bunker destroy <id> --server <host>` command it requires; a post-failure `bunker info <id> --server <host>` census shows not_found or the named command is in the output.\n5. tests/selftest.sh gains a fixture that stages node-create's file set into a temp dir and asserts no unhandled interpreter error plus the named disk-floor row; `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` at the fixed tip.","probe":"d=$(mktemp -d); mkdir -p \"$d/harness\" \"$d/home\"; cp scripts/project-node.sh scripts/lib.sh \"$d/\"; cp scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh \"$d/harness/\"; chmod +x \"$d/project-node.sh\"; HOME=\"$d/home\" \"$d/project-node.sh\" --apply --project cfprobe --harness hermes --repo https://github.com/github/gitignore; echo rc=$?","created_at":"2026-11-03T15:12:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T15:12:00Z"}
 {"id":"PERF-014","title":"P4 — after PERF-013's layer scoping the gate is 1.50 s CPU; cut the largest measured residual arm without unscoping any documented command","status":"pending","priority":"P4","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Perf tick code-factory-perf-2026-11-03-14-51-11. Post-PERF-013 measurement at e21f2ce: `bash tests/selftest.sh` = 1.50 / 1.59 / 1.49 s user+sys (34 PASS rows), from 2.58 / 2.49 / 2.49 s at d5f7fb2. Residual attribution (docs/audits/code-factory-perf-2026-11-03-PERF-013.md): section 15's two documented-shape arms 0.28 s (must stay unscoped), tests/test_lane_target.py 0.21 s + tests/test_disk_floor.py 0.04 s, section 3's --layers 00 run 0.09 s (three python3 spawns in the floor resolver), remaining fixture runs + section 13 ~0.15 s, and ~0.8 s of 9 `bash -n` launches, board JSONL parse, three boardctl invocations, README/prompt greps, per-section git init/commit/clone --bare and fetch_verified digest work. PERF-013's absolute 1.2 s target is retired here as unreachable with every assertion intact; this row asks for a measured cut, not a number.","acceptance_criteria":"1. Attribute the current gate to named arms with a re-runnable script per arm (/usr/bin/time -p, three runs each); do not quote xtrace-derived wall times as measurements (xtrace inflates them).\n2. Cut the single largest residual arm by a measured amount: before/after user+sys for the arm AND for the whole gate, three consecutive runs each, commands quoted, same tree otherwise.\n3. No assertion dropped or weakened and no documented command unscoped to buy the number: the gate keeps 34 `^  PASS` rows and SELFTEST: PASS, and the real provision still prints 3 FAIL rows -> NODE: FAIL (3).\n4. Report the honest floor with the number; if the remaining arms cannot move without weakening a check, say so and close the row on the measured attribution instead of chasing it.","probe":"cd /home/kara/code-factory && for i in 1 2 3; do /usr/bin/time -p bash tests/selftest.sh; done 2>&1 | awk '/^real|^user|^sys/{print}'; expected: SELFTEST: PASS with 34 PASS rows and user+sys per run","created_at":"2026-11-03T15:30:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T15:30:00Z","detail":{"evidence":[{"run_id":"code-factory-perf-2026-11-03-14-51-11","ts":"2026-11-03T15:30:00Z","tree_fp":"84ba918c30462747b6b430e85c12e29181ae7531","artifacts":["docs/audits/code-factory-perf-2026-11-03-PERF-013.md"]}]}}
 {"id":"CF-076","title":"P3 — a Tier-2 PASS is bound to a commit that cannot contain the graded change: PERF-013's PASS names 4720cdc (no --layers in that tree) and certifies a sub-1.2 s measurement the fix falsifies","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-11-03T15:24:53Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T15:24:53Z","detail":{"verdict_run_id":"88e945ac","graded_commit":"4720cdcd44231c8da49b061486ae7683b4f449fc","graded_commit_content":"board only: .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl + events.jsonl (the CF-075 filing)","decisive_commit":"e21f2ce790e6d595c3f13c559358e9b7817532fd","decisive_commit_contains":"scripts/project-node.sh 5 x --layers, tests/selftest.sh 7 x --layers, specs/NODE.md 2 x --layers"},"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-03 by the code-factory QA lane (tick code-factory-qa-2026-11-03-15-19-48) against origin/main 8892086.\n\n(1) .gitreins/history/2026-11-03/88e945ac/verdict.json records task PERF-013, passed=true, commit=4720cdcd4423, with one evidence item whose source is `git show 4720cdcd4423` (22477 bytes, the CF-075 board-filing patch). The feature under grade does not exist at that commit: `git show 4720cdc:scripts/project-node.sh | grep -c -- '--layers'` = 0, tests/selftest.sh = 0, specs/NODE.md = 0. The fix is the next commit: e21f2ce has 5 / 7 / 2. Four of the five graded criteria ('accepts --layers 20,40 and rejects an unknown layer', 'a scoped run prints SCOPE and SKIP and never writes node.json', 'fixtures 4/4b/14/15 use --layers', 'unscoped behaviour unchanged') are therefore unsatisfiable at the tree the verdict names.\n\n(2) The tier-2 detail cites text that is not in the graded tree: items[0] = {status: PASS, detail: 'PASS: --layers 20,40 accepted (SCOPE line printed, exit 1 from real FAIL rows); --layers 20,99 -> usage text + exit 2; --layers 20, -> exit 2; --layers \"\" -> exit 2. scripts/project-node.sh:54-80 validation.'}. At 4720cdc, lines 54-80 are the role/disk-floor block and the argument loop has no --layers case at all, so the PASS was written from the commit message rather than read from the tree.\n\n(3) Criterion 5 of the same item ('Measured: selftest user+sys below 1.2 s on three consecutive runs') is false even at the fixed tree: e21f2ce's own message reports 1.63 / 1.56 / 1.55 s user+sys, this lane re-measured 1.54 s CPU (real 1.51, user 0.70, sys 0.84; 34 PASS rows; SELFTEST: PASS at 8892086), and the board kept PERF-013 pending marked 'AC-5 unmet' (d4eec41). A numeric threshold was certified PASS without being measured.\n\n(4) The misbinding is systemic, not a one-off. Of the PASS verdicts recorded under .gitreins/history/2026-11-03/, at least three name a commit whose tree contains none of the graded change: PERF-013 -> 4720cdc (board only), CF-055 -> ddff6bc2 (board only; the adapter-counter fix is bb99a5c), CF-058 -> d15205e (verdict-bundle only; the README fix is 557ac3f). The probe below prints touches_named_path=0 for all three. CF-054 -> d088bc6 (README + selftest) and CF-032 -> 8f1e8c1 (merge) are the counter-examples that show the field CAN carry a commit containing the change.\n\nConsequence: the PASS verdict is the artifact a later lane reads before closing a row. CF-073/CF-074 exist because closures mint unresolvable fingerprints; this is the same disease one layer up — the pointer resolves to a real commit that provably cannot satisfy the criteria it certifies, so a stranger cannot falsify the PASS from the artifact. That contradicts AGENTS.md rule 10 ('the control host decides whether it is true') and docs/traceability-doctrine.md rule 4 (a closing verdict binds to a PUBLISHED commit).\n\nNot established here: whether the judge was launched with --scan-root into the worker's worktree; if it was, the tree may have been right and only the recorded pointer is wrong. Either way the artifact states what it graded and it states a tree without the change, and the repair is identical — record the graded tree explicitly and refuse a PASS that does not contain it.\n\nRe-verified in the same tick, independent of this defect: PERF-013's implementation at 8892086 gives 34 PASS rows and SELFTEST: PASS at 1.54 s CPU with 61/61 rows parse; a live 200 fixture on 127.0.0.1:8767 showed the manifest gate works in both directions (unscoped all-green writes node.json, scoped all-green writes none and prints SKIP layer 90); the unscoped output is byte-identical to 4720cdc's script apart from the live mem row; and three reintroduced defects (layer 00 stops printing FAIL for an unreachable URL, layer 40 misclassifies a missing ref as an access failure, layer 30 reports an absent crier identity as ok) each turned the scoped fixtures red, so the perf refactor did not weaken the gate.","acceptance_criteria":"1. A committed, re-runnable audit script (e.g. scripts/verdict_binding_audit.sh <history-date-dir>) prints one line per PASS verdict under .gitreins/history/<date>/*/verdict.json carrying task_id, run dir, recorded commit, whether that commit resolves in a FRESH clone of origin, and whether `git show --name-only <commit>` touches at least one path the graded row's ch:trace names (or, when the verdict carries a scan_commit, that commit). It exits 1 at origin/main 8892086 naming at least PERF-013/88e945ac, CF-055/c75a3783 and CF-058/9bd93bd8, and exits 0 once every PASS verdict names a commit containing the graded change. Paste both invocations and both exit codes into the evidence artifact.\n\n2. A PASS cannot be written without a graded-tree pointer: the judge path records scan_commit (the HEAD of the tree actually read) and that value resolves in a fresh clone of origin, and closing a row refuses a PASS whose graded pointer does not contain the change. Prove it causally in tests/selftest.sh as its own numbered section: with a fixture verdict bound to a commit lacking the change the section prints FAIL naming the task id, with a correctly bound fixture it prints PASS, and the same section FAILs on the pre-fix tree.\n\n3. PERF-013 stops resting on 88e945ac: either a re-judge at e21f2ce whose verdict names e21f2ce (or its scan_commit), or the row records verdict=none:misbound-88e945ac together with the run id, the reason and the exact re-judge command, and every PERF-013 acceptance criterion is re-runnable by a stranger at the commit the row names.\n\n4. Every evidence pointer this row keeps is repository-resolvable: the 88e945ac bundle is either committed (it is untracked today) or the row cites only committed paths, and `git cat-file -e` / `git ls-tree` succeeds for each cited run dir, commit and artifact in a fresh clone of origin.\n\n5. bash tests/selftest.sh prints SELFTEST: PASS, scripts/boardctl validate prints RESULT: OK, the board parses every line with no duplicate ids, and the closing commit is pushed with `git rev-list --count origin/main..HEAD` = 0.","probe":"cd /home/kara/code-factory\nfor spec in \"PERF-013:88e945ac:4720cdc:scripts/project-node.sh\" \"CF-055:c75a3783:ddff6bc2:scripts/project-node.sh\" \"CF-058:9bd93bd8:d15205e:README.md\"; do\n  IFS=: read -r id run commit path <<<\"$spec\"\n  v=\".gitreins/history/2026-11-03/$run/verdict.json\"\n  c=$(jq -r .commit \"$v\")\n  printf '%s run=%s passed=%s graded=%s touches_named_path=%s\\n' \\\n    \"$id\" \"$run\" \"$(jq -r .passed \"$v\")\" \"${c:0:8}\" \\\n    \"$(git show --name-only --format= \"$c\" | grep -c -x -- \"$path\")\"\ndone\necho \"layers_in_graded_tree=$(git show 4720cdc:scripts/project-node.sh | grep -c -- '--layers')\"\necho \"layers_in_fix=$(git show e21f2ce:scripts/project-node.sh | grep -c -- '--layers')\"\necho \"row_status_PERF-013=$(jq -r 'select(.id==\"PERF-013\")|.status' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl)\""}
